1
00:00:00,261 --> 00:00:02,867
Here's a phone's storage on an ordinary ruler,

2
00:00:03,368 --> 00:00:04,911
from zero to two hundred and fifty six

3
00:00:04,952 --> 00:00:05,413
gigabytes.

4
00:00:06,155 --> 00:00:08,422
A text message is about a kilobyte, a

5
00:00:08,503 --> 00:00:11,233
photo about three megabytes, and a film about

6
00:00:11,354 --> 00:00:12,216
four gigabytes.

7
00:00:12,958 --> 00:00:14,779
But on this ruler, the text and the

8
00:00:14,820 --> 00:00:17,581
photo are squashed almost onto zero, and the

9
00:00:17,702 --> 00:00:18,942
film sits near the left.

10
00:00:19,586 --> 00:00:21,164
Only the whole phone has room.

11
00:00:21,967 --> 00:00:24,211
When does an ordinary axis fail like this,

12
00:00:24,332 --> 00:00:26,896
and what kind of scale gives every file

13
00:00:26,997 --> 00:00:27,797
its own place?

14
00:00:29,559 --> 00:00:30,650
The trouble is the range.

15
00:00:31,235 --> 00:00:33,979
A kilobyte is a thousand bytes, a megabyte

16
00:00:34,041 --> 00:00:36,805
is a million, a gigabyte a billion, and

17
00:00:36,826 --> 00:00:38,808
a terabyte a million million.

18
00:00:39,572 --> 00:00:41,358
Each name is a thousand times the one

19
00:00:41,379 --> 00:00:41,660
before.

20
00:00:42,442 --> 00:00:44,262
One factor of ten is called an order

21
00:00:44,303 --> 00:00:46,644
of magnitude, so a text message and a

22
00:00:46,725 --> 00:00:48,865
full phone are more than eight orders of

23
00:00:48,906 --> 00:00:49,706
magnitude apart.

24
00:00:50,387 --> 00:00:52,768
On an ordinary axis, the biggest value sets

25
00:00:52,809 --> 00:00:55,490
the scale, so everything a thousand times smaller

26
00:00:55,791 --> 00:00:56,951
is crushed against zero.

27
00:00:57,735 --> 00:00:59,679
So space the values by their powers of

28
00:00:59,801 --> 00:01:00,702
ten instead.

29
00:01:01,325 --> 00:01:03,112
Ten to the three bytes goes at three,

30
00:01:03,596 --> 00:01:05,242
ten to the six at six, and so

31
00:01:05,324 --> 00:01:05,423
on.

32
00:01:05,926 --> 00:01:08,295
That's taking log base ten of each value.

33
00:01:08,918 --> 00:01:11,584
Now every step along the axis means multiplying

34
00:01:11,625 --> 00:01:14,190
by ten, because log of ten times a

35
00:01:14,251 --> 00:01:16,476
number is one more than the log of

36
00:01:16,497 --> 00:01:16,877
the number.

37
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:20,368
Equal steps mean equal factors, not equal differences.

38
00:01:20,991 --> 00:01:22,398
That's a logarithmic scale.

39
00:01:22,981 --> 00:01:26,592
Kilobyte, megabyte, gigabyte and terabyte sit three steps

40
00:01:26,633 --> 00:01:28,940
apart, and every file has its own place.

41
00:01:29,556 --> 00:01:30,701
So when should you use one?

42
00:01:31,307 --> 00:01:32,601
Compare two sets of data.

43
00:01:33,206 --> 00:01:35,269
The brightness of some stars runs from about

44
00:01:35,390 --> 00:01:37,795
three times ten to the minus thirteen, to

45
00:01:37,916 --> 00:01:39,518
one point four times ten to the minus

46
00:01:39,620 --> 00:01:39,779
eight:

47
00:01:40,301 --> 00:01:42,425
a factor of about forty seven thousand.

48
00:01:43,248 --> 00:01:45,374
On a log scale, they spread out nicely.

49
00:01:46,038 --> 00:01:47,702
But the heights of a class run from

50
00:01:47,883 --> 00:01:49,868
one fifty two to one eighty seven centimetres:

51
00:01:50,390 --> 00:01:52,456
a factor of only one point two three.

52
00:01:53,179 --> 00:01:55,085
A log scale adds nothing for such a

53
00:01:55,146 --> 00:01:55,848
narrow range,

54
00:01:56,351 --> 00:01:58,257
and heights are compared by differences anyway.

55
00:01:58,981 --> 00:02:00,865
So use a log scale when the values

56
00:02:00,927 --> 00:02:02,550
span several powers of ten,

57
00:02:03,133 --> 00:02:05,299
and when comparing by factors makes sense.

58
00:02:06,211 --> 00:02:07,604
Decibels are a log scale too.

59
00:02:08,289 --> 00:02:10,652
The sound level L is ten times log

60
00:02:10,694 --> 00:02:13,057
base ten of I over I nought.

61
00:02:13,900 --> 00:02:15,563
I is the intensity, the power of the

62
00:02:15,584 --> 00:02:17,949
sound arriving on each square metre, and I

63
00:02:18,010 --> 00:02:19,633
nought is ten to the minus twelve,

64
00:02:20,155 --> 00:02:22,179
a conventional reference near the threshold of hearing.

65
00:02:23,202 --> 00:02:25,726
Multiply the intensity by ten, and the log

66
00:02:25,827 --> 00:02:27,810
goes up by one, so the level goes

67
00:02:27,871 --> 00:02:28,952
up by ten decibels.

68
00:02:29,775 --> 00:02:32,600
So a factory measuring ninety five decibels, against

69
00:02:32,641 --> 00:02:34,746
a limit of eighty five, has a reading

70
00:02:34,847 --> 00:02:37,532
about twelve percent higher, but the sound isn't

71
00:02:37,634 --> 00:02:38,515
twelve percent stronger.

72
00:02:39,158 --> 00:02:41,995
Ten decibels more means ten times the intensity.

73
00:02:42,619 --> 00:02:44,607
And a three decibel rise is a factor

74
00:02:44,708 --> 00:02:47,018
of ten to the point three, about two.

75
00:02:47,850 --> 00:02:48,995
Let's compare two places.

76
00:02:49,619 --> 00:02:52,065
A library is thirty decibels, and a café

77
00:02:52,187 --> 00:02:52,727
is seventy.

78
00:02:53,451 --> 00:02:55,357
How many times more intense is the café?

79
00:02:56,302 --> 00:02:58,562
Divide each level by ten and use it

80
00:02:58,643 --> 00:02:59,243
as a power:

81
00:02:59,824 --> 00:03:01,884
the library's intensity is ten to the three

82
00:03:01,965 --> 00:03:04,366
times I nought, and the café's is ten

83
00:03:04,387 --> 00:03:05,987
to the seven times I nought.

84
00:03:06,709 --> 00:03:08,595
Divide them, and I nought cancels:

85
00:03:09,158 --> 00:03:11,868
ten to the four, ten thousand times as

86
00:03:11,909 --> 00:03:12,229
intense.

87
00:03:13,053 --> 00:03:15,218
A quicker way: the difference is forty decibels,

88
00:03:15,620 --> 00:03:17,424
and forty over ten is four.

89
00:03:17,921 --> 00:03:20,348
Earthquake magnitude can work the same way, with

90
00:03:20,448 --> 00:03:21,491
no ten in front.

91
00:03:22,172 --> 00:03:23,753
In a simplified local magnitude,

92
00:03:24,333 --> 00:03:26,875
M is log base ten of A over

93
00:03:27,035 --> 00:03:29,176
A nought, where A is the size of

94
00:03:29,216 --> 00:03:31,237
the wiggle on a seismograph, measured at the

95
00:03:31,277 --> 00:03:32,898
same distance with the same instrument,

96
00:03:33,438 --> 00:03:36,039
so each step of one means ten times

97
00:03:36,099 --> 00:03:36,660
the amplitude.

98
00:03:37,742 --> 00:03:39,950
Earthquake A is magnitude five.

99
00:03:40,594 --> 00:03:43,882
Earthquake B has a seismograph trace fifty times

100
00:03:43,983 --> 00:03:44,343
as tall.

101
00:03:45,027 --> 00:03:47,438
Its magnitude is five plus log of fifty,

102
00:03:47,881 --> 00:03:48,885
about six point seven.

103
00:03:49,609 --> 00:03:51,834
Fifty times the wiggle shows up as only

104
00:03:52,075 --> 00:03:53,859
one point seven more on the scale.

105
00:03:54,380 --> 00:03:57,625
For dilute solutions, pH is minus log base

106
00:03:57,665 --> 00:04:01,190
ten of the hydrogen ion concentration, so more

107
00:04:01,230 --> 00:04:03,133
hydrogen ions mean a lower pH.

108
00:04:04,076 --> 00:04:05,630
Each step of one is a factor of

109
00:04:05,711 --> 00:04:05,933
ten.

110
00:04:06,617 --> 00:04:09,041
A solution at pH four has ten to

111
00:04:09,061 --> 00:04:11,685
the minus four moles per litre, and pH

112
00:04:11,745 --> 00:04:13,308
seven has ten to the minus seven.

113
00:04:13,990 --> 00:04:16,417
So pH four has a thousand times the

114
00:04:16,458 --> 00:04:19,567
hydrogen ion concentration, even though the numbers differ

115
00:04:19,629 --> 00:04:20,390
by only three.

116
00:04:21,561 --> 00:04:23,747
Star magnitudes have a minus sign and a

117
00:04:23,768 --> 00:04:24,389
two point five.

118
00:04:24,931 --> 00:04:27,316
The difference in magnitudes is minus two point

119
00:04:27,376 --> 00:04:29,761
five times log of the brightness ratio.

120
00:04:30,302 --> 00:04:33,373
So brighter stars get smaller magnitudes, and five

121
00:04:33,413 --> 00:04:35,219
magnitudes is a factor of ten to the

122
00:04:35,259 --> 00:04:36,042
two, a hundred.

123
00:04:36,925 --> 00:04:39,194
A magnitude one star is a hundred times

124
00:04:39,254 --> 00:04:41,061
as bright as a magnitude six star.

125
00:04:42,108 --> 00:04:44,144
Here's one rule for all four scales.

126
00:04:44,790 --> 00:04:47,023
Each one is a number times log base

127
00:04:47,064 --> 00:04:48,150
ten of a ratio.

128
00:04:48,893 --> 00:04:50,414
To turn a step on the scale back

129
00:04:50,455 --> 00:04:52,515
into a factor, divide the step by that

130
00:04:52,576 --> 00:04:54,397
number, and raise ten to the power.

131
00:04:55,204 --> 00:04:56,577
Decibels: divide by ten.

132
00:04:57,302 --> 00:04:58,688
Earthquakes: divide by one.

133
00:04:59,453 --> 00:05:02,189
pH: divide by minus one, so the direction

134
00:05:02,291 --> 00:05:02,592
flips.

135
00:05:03,217 --> 00:05:05,666
Stars: divide by minus two point five.

136
00:05:08,338 --> 00:05:08,724
Your turn.

137
00:05:09,455 --> 00:05:11,440
A lawnmower measures ninety decibels,

138
00:05:11,942 --> 00:05:13,747
and a conversation measures sixty.

139
00:05:14,488 --> 00:05:17,229
Using L equals ten log base ten of

140
00:05:17,250 --> 00:05:18,510
I over I nought,

141
00:05:19,091 --> 00:05:21,631
how many times more intense is the lawnmower's

142
00:05:21,672 --> 00:05:21,972
sound?

143
00:05:22,854 --> 00:05:23,698
Pause here to answer.

144
00:05:34,788 --> 00:05:36,415
The difference is thirty decibels.

145
00:05:36,898 --> 00:05:37,565
Divide by ten:

146
00:05:38,194 --> 00:05:38,416
three.

147
00:05:39,183 --> 00:05:40,951
So the ratio is ten to the three,

148
00:05:41,374 --> 00:05:42,881
a thousand times as intense.

149
00:05:43,665 --> 00:05:45,369
One and a half times compares the decibel

150
00:05:45,410 --> 00:05:47,214
numbers, as if the scale were ordinary.

151
00:05:48,870 --> 00:05:49,252
Last one.

152
00:05:49,898 --> 00:05:52,323
Set A lists the populations of ten towns

153
00:05:52,445 --> 00:05:54,689
and cities, from twelve hundred to five point

154
00:05:54,731 --> 00:05:55,532
three million people.

155
00:05:56,154 --> 00:05:58,780
Set B lists daily top temperatures over a

156
00:05:58,821 --> 00:06:00,986
month, from twenty one to thirty one degrees.

157
00:06:01,730 --> 00:06:03,617
Which set suits a logarithmic scale?

158
00:06:04,121 --> 00:06:05,449
Justify using the ranges.

159
00:06:05,973 --> 00:06:06,736
Pause here to answer.

160
00:06:18,112 --> 00:06:18,439
Set A.

161
00:06:19,029 --> 00:06:21,213
Five point three million divided by twelve hundred

162
00:06:21,375 --> 00:06:23,900
is about four thousand, more than three powers

163
00:06:23,942 --> 00:06:24,181
of ten.

164
00:06:24,924 --> 00:06:27,852
Set B only spans ten degrees, and temperatures

165
00:06:27,873 --> 00:06:28,915
are compared by differences.

166
00:06:29,858 --> 00:06:32,039
It's the ratio that matters, not the size

167
00:06:32,060 --> 00:06:32,559
of the numbers:

168
00:06:33,241 --> 00:06:34,741
a million to one and a half million

169
00:06:34,922 --> 00:06:36,383
spans less than a factor of two.

170
00:06:38,348 --> 00:06:41,111
So a log scale turns equal factors into

171
00:06:41,232 --> 00:06:43,856
equal steps, and it suits data that spans

172
00:06:44,017 --> 00:06:44,918
many powers of ten.

173
00:06:45,622 --> 00:06:47,151
That's the end of the trig graphs and

174
00:06:47,213 --> 00:06:47,997
modelling videos.

